WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:06.200
Some eternally useful lessons about exorcizing
trauma and self-actualization lie just below

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:09.000
the surface of The Pope's Exorcist.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:14.090
In The Pope's Exorcist, Father Gabriele Amorth,
played by Russell Crowe, heads to Spain to

00:00:14.090 --> 00:00:18.320
help a young boy named Henry, who is possessed
after moving with his mother and sister into

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:20.050
an abandoned abbey.

00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:24.529
While performing an exorcism on Henry, Father
Amorth becomes possessed himself.

00:00:24.529 --> 00:00:28.710
After the Vasquez family is safely out of
the house and demon-free, he goes into the

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:30.789
catacombs beneath the abbey.

00:00:30.789 --> 00:00:35.000
After a battle with the help of his colleague
Father Esquibel, he forces the demon back

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:36.000
into Hell.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.750
But Father Amorth isn't the same after this
whole ordeal.

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:43.731
Viewers learn that he is remorseful for his
role in a young girl's death by suicide, but

00:00:43.731 --> 00:00:47.950
it isn't until the demon makes him relive
that event that he confesses to God again.

00:00:47.950 --> 00:00:52.180
It's a reminder that even if we initially
confess our mistakes, it sometimes takes another

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:54.030
catalyst for the message to sink in.

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:58.390
Otherwise, Father Amorth's life may have continued
in a different direction.

00:00:58.390 --> 00:01:02.870
He might have continued to overlook certain
cases where he was needed by acting pridefully.

00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:06.979
Instead, he ends up helping the church research
other locations that may have the same type

00:01:06.979 --> 00:01:08.420
of demonic presence.

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:12.378
"And his faith gave him a certain level of
courage to do the job."

00:01:12.378 --> 00:01:18.030
Before Father Amorth's arrival, Father Esquibel
shows up as Henry's condition rapidly declines.

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:21.400
However, he's the "wrong priest," according
to the demon.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:25.290
Over the course of the movie, we learn that
Father Esquibel had indiscretions and chose

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:27.460
the priesthood over the woman he loved.

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:32.705
He is a timid individual who grows into his
role as a man of faith under Father Amorth's tutelage.

00:01:32.705 --> 00:01:37.071
While Father Esquibel struggles to resist
the taunts and temptations of the demon, he

00:01:37.071 --> 00:01:41.430
ultimately overcomes his fears and helps Father
Amorth survive his possession.

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:46.210
The lesson here is that we are only as great
at something as we allow ourselves to be.

00:01:46.210 --> 00:01:50.579
Father Esquibel could've allowed his weaknesses
to prevent him from completing the exorcism.

00:01:50.579 --> 00:01:56.136
Instead, he finds strength in what he doesn't
know and allows that to fuel him on his chosen path.

00:01:56.136 --> 00:02:00.181
He also demonstrates that sometimes we are
the only ones holding ourselves back.

00:02:00.181 --> 00:02:04.470
Father Amorth believed in Father Esquibel
even when he had doubts about himself.

00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:08.520
While it took a traumatic experience for him
to reach his full potential, it is an important

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:13.915
realization for him to have, especially considering
what he chose to give up when he joined the church.

00:02:13.915 --> 00:02:19.140
The Vasquez family, consisting of mom Julia,
teenage daughter Amy, and tween son Henry,

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:21.720
move to Spain after the death of Julia's husband.

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:25.420
The abbey had been in his family, and they
inherited it upon his death.

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:28.970
They're in a tight financial bind, so they
move into the abbey with the plan to fix it

00:02:28.970 --> 00:02:30.330
up and sell it.

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:34.340
After Henry's possession and exorcism, audiences
are told that they move back to the United

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:37.610
States and that Henry makes a complete recovery.

00:02:37.610 --> 00:02:41.250
Viewers don't actually see the Vazquezes in
that better place, as The Pope's Exorcist

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:44.390
doesn't feel the need to devote screentime
to their happy ending.

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:48.780
Despite being the initial victims of the demon
and being indirectly responsible for his return

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:52.080
to the surface world, they aren't the focus
of the story.

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:56.418
Some things don't necessarily need to be spelled
out to audiences, and this is one of those cases.

00:02:56.418 --> 00:03:00.900
This can be a surprising rarity in cinema,
as producers can be worried that viewers won't

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:02.500
be able to keep up with the narrative.

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:05.706
But this ending offers a welcome change from
that habit.

00:03:05.706 --> 00:03:07.133
"I'm here to help, Julia."

00:03:07.265 --> 00:03:11.830
When the Vasquezes move into the San Sebastian
Abbey, it is run down, and they work to restore

00:03:11.830 --> 00:03:14.909
it while maintaining the original architectural
features.

00:03:14.909 --> 00:03:18.410
But considering what happens over the course
of the film, it isn't exactly in the best

00:03:18.410 --> 00:03:20.450
condition when the priests leave.

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:24.720
The church effectively raids the property,
rescuing the documents that the priests find

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:26.000
in the catacombs below.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:30.510
They also reconsecrate the land in an effort
to keep the demon back in Hell.

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:34.430
The state of the property is symbolic of both
the film's ending and where the two priests

00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:35.980
end up after the exorcisms.

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:39.500
Like the abbey, the priests made it to the
other side alive, even though there was some

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:41.540
damage inflicted along the way.

00:03:41.540 --> 00:03:45.090
For the priests, this comes in the form of
both emotional and physical distress, but

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:47.440
for the abbey it was only physical damage.

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:50.719
But, also like the priests, the abbey still
stands tall.

00:03:50.719 --> 00:03:54.650
Despite the huge flames of gas and a demon
literally trying to take over the church,

00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:59.750
it doesn't crumble under that pressure, and
neither do Fathers Amorth and Esquibel.

00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:02.240
Sometimes we have to experience pain to grow
as people.

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:07.640
Similarly, the abbey will grow metaphorically
with the church's intervention and the reconsecration.

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:11.360
Even though it won't be used as an abbey again,
it has served an important purpose that will

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:13.890
positively impact the church for years to
come.

00:04:14.576 --> 00:04:19.280
Described as the "King of Hell" by Father
Amorth, Asmodeus is the demon present within

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:20.789
the San Sebastian Abbey.

00:04:20.789 --> 00:04:26.080
Unlike demons from other exorcism films, Asmodeus
is a powerful entity that completely changes

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:28.010
the course of history in the film.

00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:33.110
Fathers Amorth and Esquibel seem to successfully
send the demon back to Hell in the final act,

00:04:33.110 --> 00:04:36.500
and the reconsecration of the land should
provide an additional barrier to keep him

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:38.390
from returning anytime soon.

00:04:38.390 --> 00:04:42.840
Sealing Asmodeus back into Hell is an excellent
representation of something we all do in our

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:45.000
lives: compartmentalization.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:49.670
When we experience emotions we aren't prepared
to deal with, we section them off in our mind

00:04:49.670 --> 00:04:54.150
until we're ready to deal with them, just
like how Henry became mute after his father's death.

00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:56.640
And that's effectively what the priests are
doing as well.

00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:00.760
The church isn't prepared to deal with a demon
this powerful, so he's instead walled off

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:02.530
until they have time to prepare.

00:05:02.530 --> 00:05:07.050
However, they do take the time to go through
the documents they gather from the abbey.

00:05:07.050 --> 00:05:10.450
Sometimes we need to do some work before we
can approach intense emotions, especially

00:05:10.450 --> 00:05:12.220
those rooted in trauma.

00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:16.190
Even if it will take a long time, like the
priests' quest to find the 100 other locations

00:05:16.190 --> 00:05:19.670
that contain similar portals to Hell, it's
usually worth it.

00:05:19.670 --> 00:05:20.670
"I call it evil."

00:05:22.736 --> 00:05:25.510
There are several factors that lead to Henry's
possession.

00:05:25.510 --> 00:05:29.389
The construction of the abbey is one of them,
as it creates an opening in the physical barrier

00:05:29.389 --> 00:05:31.160
keeping the demon at bay.

00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:34.810
But Henry's personal pain also leaves him
particularly vulnerable.

00:05:34.810 --> 00:05:39.430
He was in the car when his father died tragically
and violently, and that trauma understandably

00:05:39.430 --> 00:05:41.600
had a significant impact on him.

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:45.500
When we meet him at the beginning of the movie,
he hasn't spoken for an entire year.

00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:49.450
He's still hurting, and moving to an unfamiliar
place isn't helping.

00:05:49.450 --> 00:05:53.240
While this is a particularly drastic case,
it's important to remember that sometimes

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:57.270
while trying to process pain, we actually
make ourselves more vulnerable.

00:05:57.270 --> 00:06:00.789
We can become so focused on trying to work
through something that it can blind us to

00:06:00.789 --> 00:06:03.120
other situations that can also hurt us.

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:06.650
In Henry's case, this includes an opening
in his soul for a demon.

00:06:06.650 --> 00:06:11.150
A Latin phrase that translates to "our sins
will seek us out" is repeated throughout the

00:06:11.150 --> 00:06:15.169
film, because of its use in the documents
that discuss the history of the abbey.

00:06:15.169 --> 00:06:19.540
This phrase reminds us that someone will take
advantage of our weaknesses if we let them,

00:06:19.540 --> 00:06:23.851
just as the demon takes advantage of Father
Amorth's trauma and Henry's pain.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:29.230
While Henry's trauma is relatively fresh,
Father Amorth's has been around for much longer.

00:06:29.230 --> 00:06:33.520
We see flashbacks to his time at war, as he
watches his friends killed by German soldiers,

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:37.880
and we also see the young woman Rosaria jump
to her death in front of him.

00:06:37.880 --> 00:06:43.009
Both incidents took place many years earlier,
but both still nonetheless weigh on him heavily.

00:06:43.009 --> 00:06:46.240
Trauma doesn't automatically go away after
a certain amount of time.

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:49.410
Even many years later we can still feel intense
emotions.

00:06:49.410 --> 00:06:53.710
Seeing that depicted on screen is an important
reminder of that for all audiences, but for

00:06:53.710 --> 00:06:59.000
men in particular, whose mental health has
not always been prioritized in popular culture.

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:03.099
Father Amorth almost looks ashamed that these
events still weigh on him, and he considers

00:07:03.099 --> 00:07:06.563
it a weakness that they can still be used
against him by the demons.

00:07:06.563 --> 00:07:07.703
But he shouldn't be.

00:07:07.703 --> 00:07:11.690
There isn't a linear path to healing, and
that is shown in how Henry copes with his

00:07:11.690 --> 00:07:15.209
father's passing and how Father Amorth remembers
his time at war.

00:07:15.460 --> 00:07:18.506
"You've been played, and you took the bait."

00:07:19.249 --> 00:07:23.210
As the priests look into the history of the
San Sebastian Abbey, they discover that the

00:07:23.210 --> 00:07:28.139
last time Asmodeus walked in the human world,
it was in the body of an important priest.

00:07:28.139 --> 00:07:32.520
This allowed the demon to infiltrate the church,
ultimately leading to the Spanish Inquisition.

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:36.530
What's worse is that the church knew that
this happened, and handled it by walling the

00:07:36.530 --> 00:07:41.131
possessed priest away under the abbey and
redacting all of the information from their records.

00:07:41.131 --> 00:07:43.900
Anyone can cover something up if they are
ashamed of it.

00:07:43.900 --> 00:07:46.970
The church covered up what happened at the
abbey because they were ashamed of the role

00:07:46.970 --> 00:07:49.770
they had in one of the darkest chapters in
religious history.

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:53.720
They knew that what happened was wrong, but
they still chose to prevent the public from

00:07:53.720 --> 00:07:57.930
learning about it, as it would discredit the
church if people knew all the details.

00:07:57.930 --> 00:08:02.169
But The Pope's Exorcist makes sure to let
us know that, even when you try to keep something

00:08:02.169 --> 00:08:05.199
a secret, it will always come to light eventually.

00:08:05.199 --> 00:08:09.569
In the case of the church, it takes hundreds
of years, but the truth still eventually comes out.

00:08:09.569 --> 00:08:14.580
Their secrets are revealed because the demon
is uncaged, which leads to more possessions.

00:08:14.580 --> 00:08:17.860
If the church had been forthcoming about the
history of the abbey, the possessions of Henry

00:08:17.860 --> 00:08:20.864
and Father Amorth could've possibly been prevented.

00:08:21.181 --> 00:08:25.690
Ultimately, The Pope's Exorcist makes it clear
that our metaphorical demons will come back

00:08:25.690 --> 00:08:26.690
to haunt us.

00:08:26.690 --> 00:08:30.560
For Father Amorth, this involves guilt over
traumatic memories being used against him

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:31.909
by an actual demon.

00:08:31.909 --> 00:08:35.960
For the church, it involves that demon coming
out of the abbey in which they confined it.

00:08:35.960 --> 00:08:40.269
And for Father Esquibel, it's a matter of
his indiscretions in previous relationships.

00:08:40.269 --> 00:08:45.000
But the movie also reminds us that we don't
have to let those demons take over our life.

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:49.240
Both priests fight against what Asmodeus presents
to them and don't allow themselves to become

00:08:49.240 --> 00:08:51.290
distracted from the task at hand.

00:08:51.290 --> 00:08:55.490
Even though Father Amorth does become possessed,
he ultimately refuses to be haunted by the

00:08:55.490 --> 00:08:57.089
past any further.

00:08:57.089 --> 00:09:01.350
While it may not seem like it at first, The
Pope's Exorcist can be interpreted as a powerful

00:09:01.350 --> 00:09:05.750
message about how to handle past trauma, the
guilt over our past actions that can threaten

00:09:05.750 --> 00:09:10.590
to consume us, and how we can conquer those
demons as we work through our pain over time.

00:09:10.590 --> 00:09:14.580
That is the journey Father Amorth goes through
over the course of the film as he performs

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:15.820
the exorcisms.

00:09:15.820 --> 00:09:19.060
"My faith does not require defense."

00:09:19.696 --> 00:09:24.420
The Pope's Exorcist is based on two books
by the real Father Gabriele Amorth.

00:09:24.420 --> 00:09:28.690
In his writings, he describes his experience
as an exorcist with the Roman Catholic Church.

00:09:28.690 --> 00:09:32.410
So there's certainly potential to continue
Father Amorth's story on screen.

00:09:32.410 --> 00:09:37.580
The Pope's Exorcist takes place in 1987, just
a single year into Amorth's tenure.

00:09:37.580 --> 00:09:41.060
There are plenty of other episodes that can
be told about his life and his work, as he

00:09:41.060 --> 00:09:45.029
conducted thousands of exorcisms over the
course of his time with the church.

00:09:45.029 --> 00:09:49.460
The two books that the film is based on, An
Exorcist Tells His Story and An Exorcist:

00:09:49.460 --> 00:09:54.670
More Stories, surely include other cases and
information that could be adapted into sequels.

00:09:54.670 --> 00:09:58.769
Any potential follow-up could also show Father
Amorth's journey as one of the founders of

00:09:58.769 --> 00:10:01.920
the International Association of Exorcists
in 1990.

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:06.310
Exorcisms are still contested in the public
eye, and following the priest's journey of

00:10:06.310 --> 00:10:11.060
creating an organization for exorcists could
provide a new angle in a genre that can sometimes

00:10:11.060 --> 00:10:12.420
struggle to be original.

